   799	
   800	  /// Mass-trace detection on the integer m/z axis.
   801	  ///
   802	  /// The OpenMS detector accepts a peak when it lies within `mass_error_ppm` of a trace's RUNNING
   803	  /// centroid, which needs a tolerance comparison against every live trace and makes the cost
   804	  /// superlinear in peaks per band -- measured on dataset D: 4 bands 18:25 wall / 163 GB against 12 bands
   805	  /// 12:53 / 103 GB, i.e. the same work costs more when the bands are bigger. On a uniform integer
   806	  /// axis the candidates for a peak are found by ARITHMETIC: a live trace can only accept indices
   807	  /// within `ppmSpan` of its centroid, so bucketing by `mz >> shift` with a bucket wider than the
   808	  /// largest span puts every candidate in the peak's own bucket or its two neighbours.
   809	  ///
   810	  /// This is a DIFFERENT ALGORITHM, not a reimplementation: it will not reproduce the OpenMS
   811	  /// detector peak for peak. It is the default (`trace:detector=integer`), and must be judged on
   812	  /// identified peptides with both search engines, never on a spectrum digest. On dataset D and dataset A the
   813	  /// two detectors agree on ~85% of the union of identified peptides, and which one identifies more
   814	  /// flips with both the search engine and the file; the case for it is ~40% less memory.
   815	  ///
   816	  /// Mobility is part of the identity, as in the IM-aware OpenMS path: a candidate must also be
   817	  /// within `im_tol` of the trace's mobility. Valley splitting is NOT done here -- the existing
   818	  /// ElutionPeakDetection step runs afterwards on the traces this produces.

