   897	  /// `bnd` are the nb+1 flight-time band edges the detector will be given (bnd[0] = tlo,
   898	  /// bnd[nb] = thi+1); pass {0, 0} for the unbanded path, which wants one globally sorted list.
   899	  ///
   900	  /// [seg 2026-09-07] The seed list is SEGMENTED BY BAND and each segment sorted on its own,
   901	  /// instead of one global sort that all 12 bands then re-scan end to end. Exact, because the
   902	  /// comparator is a total order:  filter_b(sort(all)) == sort(filter_b(all))  -- the sorted
   903	  /// sequence is unique, so band b receives precisely the subsequence it used to select. What it
   904	  /// buys: the sort is the whole of this stage (two linear passes plus an O(N log N) sort of up
   905	  /// to 239e6 indices) and it was SERIAL, at 23-63 s per window on a 2-hour acquisition; as nb
   906	  /// independent sorts it runs on nb threads of the same pool. Each band also stops walking the
   907	  /// other bands' seeds.
   908	  TracePrep prepareTracing(const PeakSlab& sl, double noise, double snr, const vector<TofIdx>& bnd,
   909	                           TofIdx tlo, TofIdx thi)
   910	  {
